                      Command List

ARC         DELAY       HELP        ORTHO       SKETCH *
AREA        DIM *       ID          PAN         SNAP
ARRAY       DIST        INSERT      PLOT        SOLID
AXIS *      END         LAYER       POINT       STATUS
BASE        ENDREP      LIMITS      (QPLOT)     TABLET
BLOCK       ERASE       LINE        QUIT        TEXT
BREAK *     FILES       LIST        REDRAW      TRACE
CHANGE      FILL        LOAD        REGEN       UNITS *
CIRCLE      FILLET *    MENU        REPEAT      WBLOCK
COPY        GRID        MOVE        RESUME      ZOOM
DBLIST      HATCH *     OOPS        SHAPE       ?

  () = machine dependent    * = ADEX optional feature

Point Entry:      Absolute: x,y     Relative: @dx,dy    Distance, angle: @d<a
Object selection: L = Last object   W = Within window

Command repeat:   To repeat the last command, press space or RETURN.
\ARC
The  ARC  command draws an arc (circle segment), and accepts several
different arc specifications:

  - three points on the arc
  - start point, center, end point
  - start point, center, included angle
  - start point, center, length of chord
  - start point, end point, radius
  - start point, end point, included angle
  - start point, end point, starting direction
  - continuation of previous line or arc

3-point format:  ARC  Start point or C:  <point>
                 Second point or C or E: <point>
                 End point or R or A or L:  <point>

Options:  A = included Angle    D = starting Direction    L = Length of chord
          C = Center point      E = End point             R = Radius
          To continue previous line or arc, reply to "Start point" prompt
          with a space or RETURN

Reference:  Section 3.3 of User Guide.
\AREA
The  AREA  command calculates the area of an enclosed space.
You define the space by designating at least three points.

Format:     AREA  First point: <point>
            Next point:  <point>
            Next point:  <point>
            Next point:  ...
                         press RETURN to end point entry

Reference:  Section 6.3 of User Guide.
\ARRAY
The  ARRAY  command makes multiple copies of selected objects, in a
rectangular or circular pattern.

Format:     ARRAY 
            Select objects or Window or Last: <select>
            Rectangular or circular array (R/C):

For a rectangular array, you will be asked for the number of columns and 
rows, and the spacing between them.

For a circular array, you must supply a center point, the angle between
items, and the number of items (or the angle to be covered).  If only
one object is being replicated and that object is a Block, it may 
optionally be rotated about its insertion point at each step.

Reference:  Section 6.2 of User Guide
\AXIS
The  AXIS  command controls the display of axes, or ruler lines,
along the edge of the graphics display.  This is an Advanced Drafting
Extensions feature.

Format:     AXIS  On/Off/Tick Spacing(X): 

The axis display may be turned on and off, and the tick spacing may be
specified in terms of drawing units or as a multiple of the Snap
resolution.  For example, a value of "5X" would place a tick mark
at every fifth Snap point.

Reference:  Section 9.1 of User Guide
\BASE
The  BASE  command is defines a reference point for insertion
and rotation of the current drawing in subsequent drawings.

Format:     BASE  Base point:  <point>

Reference:  Section 7.1 of User Guide.
\BLOCK
The  BLOCK  command allows you to name a group of objects which can
then be INSERTed as a unit anywhere in the current drawing, with 
specified X and Y scales and rotation.

Format:     BLOCK  Block name:  <name>
            Insertion base point:  <point>
            Select objects or Window or Last:  <select>

Reference:  Section 7.2 of User Guide.
\BREAK
The  BREAK  command deletes part of a line, trace, circle, or arc.
This is an Advanced Drafting Extensions feature.

Format:     BREAK  Point to object: <point>
            Enter second point: <point>

A line, trace, or arc can be broken into two lines, or one end can be
cut off.  A circle will be changed into an arc by deleting the portion
from the first point to the second, going counterclockwise.

Reference:  Section 9.4 of User Guide
\CHANGE
The  CHANGE  command allows you to modify the properties of
existing entities in the drawing.

Format:     CHANGE   Select objects or Window or Last:  <select>
            Intersection point/L:  <point>

The following entity types may be modified (IP = intersection point):

   Line   - Endpoint closest to IP changes to IP (ORTHO can affect this).
   Circle - Radius changes so that IP is on circumference.
   Block  - Location changes to IP.  New angle may be specified.
   Text   - Location changes to IP.  New angle and text may be specified.

Any entity's layer number may be changed by replying "L" when prompted
for the intersection point.

Format:     CHANGE  Select objects or Window or Last:  <select>
            Intersection point/L:  L
            New layer:  <layer number>

Reference:  Section 6.2 of User Guide.
\CIRCLE
The CIRCLE command is used to draw a circle.  You may specify the circle
using a center point and radius.

Format:     CIRCLE  Center point (or 3P or 2P):  <point>
            Radius (or D):  <radius value>

When specifying the radius, you may enter a point rather than a value.
The designated point will be on the circumference.  If it is more
convenient to enter the diameter than the radius, reply to the "Radius"
prompt with a "D".

The circle may also be specified using three points on the
circumference (reply "3P" when prompted for the center point),
or by designating two endpoints of its diameter (reply "2P").

3-point format:   CIRCLE  Center point (or 3P or 2P):  3P
                  First point:  <point>
                  Second point: <point>
                  Third point:  <point>

Reference:  Section 3.2 of User Guide.
\COLOR
\COLORS
Drawing colors are controlled by the LAYER command.

Color numbers 1 through 7 have standard meanings as follows:

         1 - Red
         2 - Yellow
         3 - Green
         4 - Cyan
         5 - Blue
         6 - Magenta
         7 - White

Reference:  LAYER command, Section 5.4 of User Guide
\COPY
The  COPY  command is used to duplicate an existing drawing entity
at another location without erasing the original.

Format:     COPY  Displacement:  <1st point, or x-y distance>
            Second point:  <2nd point, or RETURN>
            Select objects or Window or Last:  <select>

Reference:  Section 6.2 of User Guide.
\DBLIST
The  DBLIST  command produces a complete list of the contents
of the drawing database for the current drawing.  This command
is used mostly for debugging.

Format:     DBLIST

Reference:  Section 6.3 of User Guide.
\DELAY
The  DELAY  command is used in command scripts to allow the display
to be viewed before the next command is automatically issued.

Format      DELAY <number>

The larger the number, the longer the delay.

Reference:  Command scripts, Section 8.4 of User Guide
\DIM
\DIMENSION
The  DIM  command is used to add dimension notations to a drawing.  It
measures the horizontal or vertical distance between designated points,
and draws arrows, witness lines, and dimension text.  This is an
Advanced Drafting Extensions feature.

Formats:    DIM  First extension line origin (or ABCT):  <point> *
            Dimension line intersection:  <point>
            Second extension line origin:  <point>
            Dimension text:  <text or RETURN>

         *  When prompted for the first extension line origin, you
            may enter any of the following options:

            A       = Set arrow size
            B       = Continue from 1st extension line
            C       = Continue from 2nd extension line
            T       = Set dimension text orientation

Reference:  Section 9.6 of User Guide.
\DIST
The  DIST  command displays the distance (in drawing units)
between two designated points.

Format:     DIST  First point:  <point>
            Second point: <point>

If a single number is entered in response to the "First point:" prompt,
DIST will simply display that number in the current UNITS format
(Advanced Drafting Extensions feature).

Reference:  Section 6.3 of User Guide.
\END
The  END  command causes AutoCAD to exit from the Drawing Editor
(after saving the updated version of the current drawing), and
returns you to the Main Menu.  If you then wish to exit AutoCAD
entirely, select item 0 from the Main Menu.

Format:     END

Reference:  Section 2.10 of User Guide.
\ENDREP
The  ENDREP  command allows you to make a rectangular pattern of the
objects drawn since the last  REPEAT  command.  

Format:     ENDREP  Columns:  <integer>
            Rows:  <integer>
            Column distance:  <value or point>
            Row distance:     <value or point>

See the ARRAY command for another (preferred) method of creating
patterns.

Reference:  Section 6.2 of User Guide.
\ERASE
The  ERASE  command lets you delete selected entities from the drawing.

Format:     ERASE  Select objects or Window or Last:  <select>

You can easily erase just the last object you drew by responding to
the "Select objects" prompt with "L".  

The  OOPS  command may be used to retrieve the last thing you erased.

Reference:  Section 6.2 of User Guide.
\FILES
The  FILES  command is used to gain access to disk file directories.

Format:     FILES

This invokes the File Utility menu, which displays a list of
subtasks.  Using this menu, you may list the names of files on disk,
delete selected files, or rename a file.

Reference:  Section 8.3 of User Guide
\FILL
The  FILL  command controls whether solids and traces are to be
solid-filled or just outlined.

Formats:    FILL ON    = Solids and traces filled
            FILL OFF   = Solids and traces outlined

Reference:  Section 4.6 of User Guide.
\FILLET
The  FILLET  command connects two lines with a smooth arc of specified 
radius.  The two lines will first be extended until they intersect,
and then they will be trimmed until they meet the arc precisely.
This is an Advanced Drafting Extensions feature.

Format:     FILLET  Select two lines or R: <select>

Respond "R" if you want to set the fillet radius.  The fillet radius is
remembered as part of the drawing, and will be used in subsequent FILLET
commands until you change it again.  The initial value of the fillet radius
is zero; a zero radius results in the two lines intersecting precisely.

Reference:  Section 9.5 of User Guide
\GRID
The  GRID  command controls the display of a grid of alignment dots
which may assist in the placement of objects in the drawing.

Formats:    GRID ON       - turns grid on with previous spacing
            GRID OFF      - turns grid off
            GRID number   - sets grid spacing in drawing units (zero
                            causes it to equal the Snap resolution)
            GRID numberX  - sets spacing to multiple of Snap resolution

Reference:  Section 5.2 of User Guide.
\HATCH
The  HATCH  command is used to crosshatch or pattern-fill an area.
This is an Advanced Drafting Extensions feature.

Format:     HATCH  Pattern (name,style / U / ?): <enter name and style>

If you reply with a standard pattern, AutoCAD will ask for a scale and
angle to be applied to the pattern.  "?" will list the standard patterns
on file, and "U" prompts you to define a pattern on the fly:

            Angle for crosshatch lines:  <angle>
            Spacing between lines:  <distance>
            Double hatch area (Y/N) <N>:

The styles available are:

            N = Normal (the default)
            O = Outermost area only
            I = Ignore internal structure

Reference:  Section 9.7 of User Guide
\HELP
\?
The  HELP  (or "?")  command causes AutoCAD to display help information.

Formats:    HELP (or ?)
            Command name (RETURN for list): <command name or RETURN>

If a <command name> is given, information about that command will be
displayed.  Otherwise, the display will consist of a list of AutoCAD
commands, and a brief reminder of the methods of point specification.

Reference:  Section 2.9 of User Guide.
\ID
The  ID  command displays the coordinates of a designated point
in the drawing.

Format:     ID  Point:  <point>

Reference:  Section 6.3 of User Guide.
\INSERT
The  INSERT  command copies an existing drawing file into the current
drawing at a designated point, applying scale factors and rotation.

Format:     INSERT  File name:  <file>
            Insertion point:  <point>
            X scale factor (default = 1):  <value>
            Y scale factor (default = 1):  <value>
            Rotation angle:  <angle>

Normally, the named drawing file will be copied into the current
drawing as a "block" which can later be manipulated as a whole.
However, if the file name is preceded by "*", the individual
components of the inserted drawing will be retained.

The X and Y scale factors may be specified simultaneously by using
the insertion point as the lower left corner of a box, and a new point
as the upper right corner.  To do this, just enter the new point in
response to the "X scale factor" prompt.

Reference:  Section 7.1 of User Guide.
\LAYER
The  LAYER  command allows you to control which drawing layer you
are currently drawing on, and which drawing layers are to be displayed,
If you are using a color display, you also use  LAYER  to define the
color of each drawing layer.

Formats:    LAYER number       =  Set current layer number
            LAYER ON n,n,...   =  Turn on layers
            LAYER OFF n,n,...  =  Turn off layers
            LAYER COLOR x      =  Set layer color
            LAYER ?            =  List layers and colors

Reference:  Section 5.4 of User Guide.
\LIMITS
The  LIMITS  command allows you to change the upper and lower limits
of the drawing area while working on a drawing.

Format:     LIMITS
            Old lower left corner   X =    0.0000  Y =   0.0000
            Enter new lower left corner or RETURN:  <enter>
            Old upper right corner  X =   10.0000  Y =  10.0000
            Enter new upper right corner or RETURN:  <enter>

Reference:  Section 4.8 of User Guide.
\LINE
The  LINE  command allows you to draw straight lines.

Format:     LINE  From point:  <point>
            To point:  <point>
            To point:  <point>
            To point:  RETURN to end line sequence

You can continue the previous line or arc by responding to the
"From point:" prompt with a space or RETURN.  If you are drawing
a sequence of lines which will become a closed polygon, you may
reply to the "to point" prompt with "C" to draw the last segment
(close the polygon).

Lines may be constrained to horizontal or vertical by the ORTHO command.

Reference:  Section 3.1 of User Guide.
\LIST
The  LIST  command displays database information about selected
objects.

Format:     LIST  Select objects or Window or Last:  <select>

Reference:  Section 6.3 of User Guide.
\LOAD
The  LOAD  command is used to load Shape or Text Font definitions
from a disk file.

Format:     LOAD  File name:  <file>

No file type should be specified on the LOAD command:  type ".SHP"
is assumed by AutoCAD.

Reference:  Sections 3.7 and 7.3 of User Guide.
\MENU
The  MENU  command is used to load a new set of commands into the
screen menu and tablet menu from a disk file.

Format:     MENU  File name:  <file>

If you respond with a space or RETURN (null file name), the screen
menu will be erased.

Reference:  Section 8.1 of User Guide.
\MOVE
The  MOVE  command allows you to erase an existing entity or group of
entities, and re-insert them at a different point in the drawing.

Format:     MOVE  Displacement:  <1st point, or x-y distance>
            Second point:  <2nd point, or RETURN>
            Select objects or Window or Last:  <select>

Reference:  Section 6.2 of User Guide.
\OOPS
The  OOPS  command re-inserts the object or objects which were deleted
by the most recent ERASE command.

Format:     OOPS

Reference:  Section 6.2 of User Guide.
\ORTHO
The  ORTHO  command allows you to control AutoCAD's "orthogonal"
drawing mode.  All lines and traces drawn while this mode is on will
be constrained to be horizontal or vertical.

Formats:    ORTHO ON     = Turn orthogonal mode on.
            ORTHO OFF    = Turn orthogonal mode off.

Reference:  Section 5.3 of User Guide.
\PAN
The  PAN  command allows you to move the display window in any
direction, without changing its magnification.  This lets you
see details which are currently off the screen.

You can specify a relative movement, as in:

Format:     PAN  From - to:  <relative coordinates>
            Second point:  <RETURN>

Or you can designate two points to specify the displacement you wish.

Format:     PAN  From - to:  <point>
            Second point:  <point>

Reference:  Section 4.4 of User Guide.
\PLOT
The  PLOT  command causes a hard copy of the drawing (as most recently
displayed) to be produced on the plotter.

Format:     PLOT

Reference:  Chapter 10 of User Guide.
\POINT
The  POINT  command permits you to place a point in the drawing.
A point will plot as a dot the width of the pen tip your plotter uses.

Format:     POINT  Point:  <designate point>

Reference:  Section 3.8 of User Guide.
\QPLOT
The  QPLOT  command (not available on some machines) causes a
rough-draft hard copy of the screen display to be produced on an
Epson dot-matrix printer.

Format:     QPLOT

Reference:  Section 10.5 of User Guide.
\QUIT
The  QUIT  command causes AutoCAD to exit from the Drawing Editor,
discarding all updates to the current drawing, and returns you
to the Main Menu.  If you then wish to exit AutoCAD entirely,
select item 0 from the Main Menu.

Format:     QUIT  Really want to discard all changes?  Y

If you reply with anything other that "Y" or "YES", the QUIT command
will be ignored, and you will remain in the Drawing Editor.

Reference:  Section 2.10 of User Guide.
\REDRAW
The  REDRAW  command causes the display screen to be redrawn,
eliminating any point entry "blips" from the display.

Format:     REDRAW

Reference:  Section 4.5 of User Guide.
\REGEN
The  REGEN  command forces AutoCAD to regenerate the entire
drawing and redraw it on the screen.

Format:     REGEN

Reference:  Section 4.7 of User Guide.
\REPEAT
The REPEAT command specifies that the following objects being drawn (up to the
next ENDREP command) are to be grouped to form a rectangular pattern, or
"array".  See the ENDREP command for further details.

Format:     REPEAT

Reference:  Section 6.2 of User Guide.

Also see the  ARRAY  command for an alternate method of constructing
rectangular (and circular) patterns.
\RESUME
The  RESUME  command may be used to return to a command script
which has been interrupted due to an error or keyboard input.

Format:     RESUME

Reference:  Command scripts, Section 8.4 of User Guide
\SNAP
The "snap resolution" is the spacing of an imaginary grid of dots
with which newly designated points must align.  The  SNAP  command
allows you to change the snap resolution at any time, or to turn it
off entirely for "free-hand" drawing.

Format:     SNAP number  = Set alignment spacing
            SNAP ON      = Align designated points
            SNAP OFF     = Do not align designated points

Reference:  Section 5.1 of User Guide.
\SHAPE
The  SHAPE  command inserts a defined shape into the drawing,
assuming that the shape definitions have been loaded using the
LOAD command.

Format:     SHAPE  Name:  <shape name>
            Starting point:  <point>
            Height:  <value>
            Angle:  <angle>

Reference:  Section 7.3 of User Guide.
\SKETCH
The  SKETCH  command allows you to do freehand drawings.  This is
an Advanced Drafting Extensions feature, and requires a pointing
device such as a digitizing tablet, mouse, or light pen.

Format:     SKETCH
            Record increment: <value>
            Sketch.  Pen eXit Quit Record Erase Connect .

Subcommands:  P = Raise/lower sketching pen
              X = Record temporary lines, and exit Sketch
              Q = Discard temporary lines, and exit Sketch
              R = Record temporary lines, but remain in Sketch
              E = Erase temporary lines from a specified point to the end
              C = Connect:  restart sketch at last end point
              . = Draw line from end to current point (pen up)

Reference:  Section 9.8 of User Guide
\SOLID
The  SOLID  command allows you to draw solid filled regions by
entering them as quadrilateral or triangular sections.

Format:     SOLID  First point:  <point>
            Second point:  <point>
            Third point:   <point>
            Fourth point:  <point, or RETURN for triangular section>
            Third point:   <point, or RETURN to end solid>

Reference:  Section 3.5 of User Guide.
\STATUS
The  STATUS  command produces several lines on the display,
denoting the current drawing extents and the current settings
of various drawing modes and parameters.

Format:     STATUS

Reference:  Section 5.5 of User Guide.
\TABLET
The  TABLET  command is used when an existing hard copy drawing
is to be "copied" with a digitizing tablet.

Formats:    TABLET ON      = Turn tablet mode on
            TABLET OFF     = Turn tablet mode off
            TABLET CAL     = Calibrate tablet

Reference:  Section 8.2 of User Guide.
\TEXT
The  TEXT  command allows you to add text of any desired size
and angle to your drawing.

Format:     TEXT  Starting point (or ACR):  <point>
            Height:  <value or two points>
            Angle:  <angle or point>
            Text:   <text string be be drawn>

If you enter a point for the "Starting point", the text will be
drawn left-justified at that point.  Alternatively, you may reply:

    A - To align the text between two designated end points.
        Height and Angle will not be requested in this case.
    C - To center the text around a specified point.
    R - To right-justify the text at a designated end point.

If a TEXT command is repeated (by just pressing space or RETURN),
the new text will be placed below the previous line, with the same
height, angle, and alignment as the first text line.

Reference:  Section 3.6 of User Guide.
\TRACE
The  TRACE  command allows you to draw traces (solid-filled lines
of specified width).

Format:     TRACE  From point:  <point>
            To point:  <point>
            To point:  <point>
            To point:  <point>
            To point:  RETURN to end trace entry

Traces may be constrained to horizontal or vertical by the ORTHO command.

Reference:  Section 3.4 of User Guide.
\UNITS
The  UNITS  command instructs AutoCAD to display coordinates and distances
in any of four formats, and to accept input in these formats.  This is an
Advanced Drafting Extensions feature.

Format:     UNITS

A menu will be displayed, allowing you to select one of the following
display/input formats (assume a distance of 15.5 drawing units is to
be displayed):

            Scientific        1.55E+01
            Decimal           15.5000
            Engineering       1'-3.5"
            Architectural     1'-3 1/2"

You may also specify the precision of the display (the number of digits
after the decimal point, or the smallest fraction of an inch to display).

Reference:  Section 9.2 of User Guide
\WBLOCK
The WBLOCK command writes all or part of a drawing out to a disk file.

Format:     WBLOCK  File name: <output file name>
            Block name: <see below>

No file type should be specified; type ".DWG" is assumed.
The different responses to the "Block name" prompt are:

            name      - The named block will be written to the disk file.
            =         - Same as above, but the block name is the same
                        as the file name.
            *         - The entire drawing will be written to disk.  Useful
                        for periodic saves of the drawing, but note that any
                        unreferenced Block definitions will not be written.
            <blank>   - Permits selection of individual objects to be written
                        to disk.  Also requests an insertion base point.

Reference:  Section 7.2 of User Guide.
\ZOOM
The ZOOM command magnifies the drawing on the display screen (to see more
detail) or shrinks it (to view more or the drawing with less detail).

Formats:    ZOOM number  = Magnification relative to ZOOM All display
                           (ZOOM All = ZOOM 1).  Higher numbers magnify,
                           lower numbers shrink.
            ZOOM numberX = Magnification relative to current display (1X).
            ZOOM A       = All (place entire drawing -- all visible
                           layers -- on display at once).
            ZOOM C       = Center (designate desired center point and
                           new display height).
            ZOOM E       = Extents (displays current drawing content as 
                           large as possible).
            ZOOM L       = Lower left corner (designate desired lower
                           left corner and new display height).
            ZOOM P       = Previous (restore previous view).
            ZOOM W       = Window (designate rectangular area to
                           be drawn as large as possible).

Reference:  Section 4.3 of User Guide.
